{
  "image_name": "5ac575a56fb86ab28964f8357fc997d7.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed signaling pathway linking peripheral nerve injury to activation of spinal microglia. An injured DRG neuron is shown producing more Csf1 after IL‑1β/NF‑κB signaling, apparently influenced by satellite glial cells. CSF1 is then transported anterogradely along the sensory neuron to its central terminals, where it acts on nearby microglia.\n\nAt the center, a normal ramified microglial cell is contrasted with clustered, enlarged microglia, illustrating microgliosis. The enlarged schematic on the right suggests that neuron-derived CSF1 binds microglial CSF1R, driving proliferation, then microgliosis, and finally a functional active state. This activation appears to involve DAP12 and possibly SFK signaling, with a tentative link to TREM2 indicated by question marks, suggesting uncertainty in that branch.\n\nThe downstream consequence is altered microglial gene expression. The highlighted genes include inflammatory and microglial-response markers such as Tnfa, Il1b, Bdnf, Ctss, P2rx4, Cx3cr1, Irf8/5, and Dap12. Overall, the figure conveys that CSF1 released from injured sensory neurons is a key trigger that expands and reprograms spinal microglia into an activated, functionally important state after injury.",
